{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1559080-why-are-ghouls-called-ghouls","id":1559080,"title":"Why are ghouls called ghouls?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-03-28T21:28:50-07:00","id":1559080,"title":"Why are ghouls called ghouls?","content":"Vampiric &quot;ghouls&quot; are so different from the mythological ghoul. Why are they named that?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-28T23:36:30-07:00","id":1559084,"title":"","content":"They technically feed off the dead, in the form of Vitae. They perform ghoulish tasks like getting rid off bodies. And in the 19th century people who stole corpses were certainly nicknamed ghouls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2198-malkavian87","name":"Malkavian87","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2198_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-29T01:09:45-07:00","id":1559086,"title":"","content":"VtM (and the rest of the WoD) are a fairly wild mishmash of names drawn from global mythological sources. While sometimes the authors have explained specific names or naming concepts... for the most part why specific terms were used is going to be speculation at best.<br /> <br /> My best guess would be a combination of some of the early French gothic horror vampire novels using &quot;goule&quot; as the feminine counterpart to a masculine &quot;vampire.&quot; Given how much VtM ghouls seem to combine aspects of Lucy during her transformation into a vampire (a much longer process in the <i>Dracula</i> novel than VtM uses) and Renfield, it's not a huge reach to speculate that the extant Western pop culture concepts of ghouls mixed with those two very specific characters and an existing specific connection to pop culture vampires could combine in some fashion to the naming choice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-29T06:15:38-07:00","id":1559089,"title":"","content":"It comes from the Arabic word Ghul, meaning a cannibalistic corpse. Also, in D&amp;D where the undead are organized in a sort of hierarchy, I remember seeing a vampire with a bunch of ghoul minions when I played as a teen before Vampire the Masquerade came out (though that might\u2019ve been homebrew on the part of the DM). The original Ghuls appear in the V5 book Tattered Facade, but I haven\u2019t read their writeup yet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-29T11:11:54-07:00","id":1559095,"title":"","content":"Ghuls in Arabic folklore had the quality of being hidden threats. Something that seems human and friendly, until it strikes. There seem to be few legends of corpse eating ghuls in actual Arab folklore. Ghuls are shape-shifting goblins, something related to the Jinni but not properly a jinn. <br /> <br /> The few Arab tales involving corpse eating ghuls got to France in the early 18th century. There ghuls lost their disguise powers and became corpse eating undead ghouls. <br /> <br /> V:tM simply made an interesting use of the terminology.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-29T11:20:58-07:00","id":1559097,"title":"","content":"Ghuls in pre-Islamic folklore is a much more generic monster figure that's very hard to define as we have so many contradictory stories from early myths. The Western pop-culture interpretation - as seen in D&amp;D but hardly originally to it - can be traced pretty directly to Antoine Gallad's partial translation of <i>Arabian Nights</i> (not partial because he didn't finish translating what he had, but only because he had access to part of the collection). It was raided pretty heavily by European horror and fantasy writers during the 1800s because it added a lot of novel creatures to mix in and didn't really explain much about them since the source was aimed at an audience that already knew the myths. That's also how we got the idea of a genie was a mischievous wish-granting spirit instead of the more fully developed djinn mythology in Middle Eastern cultures.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-29T14:41:42-07:00","id":1559102,"title":"","content":"I think it's worth mentioning that the 1e core mentions:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There exists a class of creature in between mortal and Vampire, which the Kindred have named Ghoul. It is not the legendary corpse-eating gh\u00fbl of the Indies, although certain individuals may display similar behaviour. </div> </div> </div>Think it's interesting that such an early book deliberately points out that the word doesn't really make sense on its face.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-03T23:35:28-07:00","id":1559211,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malkavian87</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1559080-why-are-ghouls-called-ghouls?p=1559084#post1559084\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They technically feed off the dead, in the form of Vitae. They perform ghoulish tasks like getting rid off bodies. And in the 19th century people who stole corpses were certainly nicknamed ghouls.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would add that those associations with the word seem to have sprung in the 18th century when the word ghoul as such was introduced to french and then english languages, but your point is pretty much spot on. Such temporality can mean that its usage before the 1700s to refer to kindred retainers would be anachronistic but then it all becomes a matter of how one feels like about playing with the changing and evolution of vampiric slang through the centuries in one's table and maybe coming with an &quot;old parlance&quot; alternate term, that is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}